<p class="lead">As the name suggests, Champion Forest is defined by its trees. Many homes in the area sit on wooded lots beneath large pines and hardwoods, with deep shade, natural leaf litter and a canopy that frames the house. Spring Landscape Lighting designs systems here that treat the forest as the main subject, using light to reveal trunks, canopies and the spaces between them, while keeping the setting dark, quiet and natural.</p>

<h2>Lighting under a big canopy</h2>
<p>Large canopy trees change every lighting decision. They block light from above, cast deep shadows and give you dramatic vertical structure to work with. A few approaches tend to work best on wooded lots:</p>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Selective uplighting</h3><p>Choose a few standout trunks near the house or drive and light them well. Lighting every tree flattens the woods.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Moonlighting</h3><p>Fixtures mounted high in mature trees cast dappled light over lawns, beds and paths. On a wooded lot it often looks like the most natural light on the property.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Depth layering</h3><p>Soft light on trees farther back in the lot adds depth, so the yard does not end in a black wall at the edge of the lawn.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Tall pines need narrow beams to reach the canopy, while broad hardwoods often look best cross-lit from two or three sides. Our <a href="/services/tree-garden-lighting/">tree and garden lighting</a> service and the <a href="/tree-uplighting-guide/">tree uplighting guide</a> go into more detail.</p>

<h2>Practical realities of wooded lots</h2>
<p>Trees bring maintenance. Needles and leaves pile onto fixture lenses, shade slows down drying after rain, and roots spread across most of the yard. We plan for all of it:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fixtures with shrouds or angled lenses that shed debris, placed where they are easy to clear.</li>
  <li>Wire routed and hand dug around major roots instead of trenching straight through.</li>
  <li>In-tree fixtures on growth-tolerant mounting hardware, checked periodically.</li>
  <li>Layouts that account for storm risk, so a fallen limb does not take out an entire zone.</li>
</ul>
<p>Shade also means planting beds often rely on ferns, hostas and other shade plants with interesting texture, which respond well to low, close accent lighting.</p>

<h2>Paths and arrival</h2>
<p>Wooded lots can be very dark at night. Walks and drives under a canopy need dependable <a href="/services/pathway-driveway-lighting/">pathway lighting</a>, though often less of it than you might expect when moonlighting is already washing the ground. Combining the two gives safe footing without a row of visible fixtures.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Want a rough idea of how many fixtures your lot might need? Try the <a href="/tools/fixture-estimator/">fixture estimator</a>, then let us refine it on site.</div>

<p>Spring Landscape Lighting is happy to walk your property at dusk so you can see which trees respond best to light.</p>

